-- statement:
--   Case 1: Corner N\nIf the N is in a corner, there is $1$ choice for the next $O$ , three choices for the second $O$ , and four choices for the last $N$ . Since there are four Ns in a corner, there are $4 \times 1 \times 3 \times 5 = 60$ ways to do this.
-- source:
